{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-66012" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=1$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=5$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{4}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-66012" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-66012" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9abae88a618709fe5f6a35311aa202d6084f3fb21033dcec89e1c25f8c1e73bf"} {"question" "q-66012" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-66012" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-66012" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-76490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=6$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-76490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{6})=1.403\\,348…≈1.403\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.403\\,3$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=1.403\\,3.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=1.403\\,3.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.403\\,3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/assets/9b3d60be42ec916f0e62f3a86cb1ce2aa628482c97f68f0df7c6ab533b919102.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -1.403\\,3 \\\\  & ≈4.879\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈1.403\\,3\\,\\cdot 4.879\\,9≈6.8\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-76490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9abae88a618709fe5f6a35311aa202d6084f3fb21033dcec89e1c25f8c1e73bf"} {"question" "q-76490" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-76490" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4.9"] ["math" "5.8"] ["math" "6.2"] ["math" "6.8"] ["math" "7.4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-76490" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6.8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-76499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-2\\pi <x<0,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-76499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ is not in the domain $-2\\pi <x<0.$ To get the first solution within the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/assets/afb20de50d2fa92c6550d1e5357b2aaf07311177e3ed82e1a15facccf2c0f0db.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value does not lie in the required domain. To get the solution that's within the given domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{7\\pi }{6},-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{x}_{2}=-\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{35{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-76499" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9abae88a618709fe5f6a35311aa202d6084f3fb21033dcec89e1c25f8c1e73bf"} {"question" "q-76499" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-76499" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{35{\\pi }^{2}}{6}"] ["math" "-\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] ["math" "\\frac{35{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] ["math" "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] ["math" "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-76499" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{35{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-324396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=5$ for $0\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-324396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(\\frac{1}{5})≈{78.463}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈{78.463}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈{78.463}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/assets/e05c5b50dc8308d278cb6dac3408349da1865d37601003e0ee3025c937404239.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈{360}^{∘}-{78.463}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{281.537}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=78.5\\,{\\,}^{∘}$\nand $x=281.5\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-324396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9abae88a618709fe5f6a35311aa202d6084f3fb21033dcec89e1c25f8c1e73bf"} {"question" "q-324396" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-324396" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "78.5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-324396" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-324396" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "281.5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-325113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-2$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x<{180}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-325113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arccos\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})={120}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $x={120}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={120}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={180}^{∘}-{120}^{∘}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/assets/29560b074afbb7d4e0cde3c1964e14696ef9879618e42c0b0c41f478e7ffcb72.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the third quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={240}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-120\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=120\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-325113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9abae88a618709fe5f6a35311aa202d6084f3fb21033dcec89e1c25f8c1e73bf"} {"question" "q-325113" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-325113" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-120"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-325113" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"} {"question" "q-325113" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "120"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041448/state.json" "file_sha256" "40872a8c4e8479b487db2b10cdab6443ad4faa001d9aeeb926f7b3b87d146f86"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1497 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
